.TH V7MOUNT 1 "August 2026" "v7fuse 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
v7mount \- mount a Seventh Edition (V7) Unix filesystem image
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B v7mount
[\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-f\fR] [\fB\-d\fR]
.I image mountpoint
.br
.B v7mount
.B \-c
.I image
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B v7mount
mounts a Seventh Edition (V7) Unix filesystem image (as used by a PDP-11) as
a FUSE filesystem, so files can be copied on and off the image for use with a
simulator such as SIMH
.BR pdp11 (1).
.PP
The image is laid out exactly as the V7 kernel expects it: a 512-byte block
size, the PDP-11's middle-endian byte order, and the same free-block/free-inode
allocation discipline as the kernel's
.IR sys/alloc.c .
The on-disk format is understood by a running V7 kernel, so files staged with
.B v7mount
are visible after booting the image.
.PP
The first form mounts the image at
.IR mountpoint .
The second form
.RB ( \-c )
does not mount; it walks the free list and the inode table and reports any
integrity problems on standard output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-r
Mount read-only.
.TP
.B \-f
Stay in the foreground (do not daemonize).
.TP
.B \-d
Enable FUSE debug output (implies
.BR \-f ).
.TP
.B \-c
Run the integrity check instead of mounting.  The image is opened read-only
and the free list and inode table are walked; a non-zero exit status means
problems were found.
.SH LOCKING
For the whole duration of a mount,
.B v7mount
holds an exclusive
.BR flock (2)
on the image file.  This is the hook a patched simulator uses to pause its own
disk I/O while the image is mounted, so the two writers never touch the image
at once.  Do not run the simulator while the image is mounted: the running
kernel caches the superblock free list, the inode table, and the buffer cache,
and editing the image behind it leaves those stale.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
mkdir mnt
v7mount rp06-0.disk mnt
cp host.txt mnt/tmp/            # copy a file onto the image
cp mnt/etc/passwd .             # copy a file off
fusermount3 -u mnt              # unmount

v7mount -c rp06-0.disk          # free-list / inode-table check
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; non-zero on error, or (for
.BR \-c )
when integrity problems were found.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fusermount3 (1),
.BR mount.fuse3 (8),
.BR flock (2),
.BR pdp11 (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Written for the v7fuse project.
